+          <h2 id="ol-energy-title">Energy per operation</h2>
+          <p>One operation is one assumed clock slot. Optical energy is power divided by slot rate. The ring edge estimate is ½CV² with 20 fF per junction. Mean switching assumes independent equiprobable data bits and three capacitive loads. The receivers and drivers stay powered.</p>
+          <dl class="ol-grid ol-metrics" id="ol-optical-budget"></dl>
+          <dl class="ol-grid ol-metrics" id="ol-electrical-budget"></dl>
+          <details><summary>Edit energy assumptions</summary><div class="ol-grid" id="ol-energy-controls"></div></details>
+          <p class="muted">The system estimate adds laser electricity, ring switching, two drivers, three receivers and entered static overhead. Laser electricity already supplies the launched optical energy. The optical account shows probe losses separately. Laser efficiency represents conversion to launched light at the three model input planes. Gate selection is modeled as fixed wiring for each run. Configuration changes, packaging and host I/O need a separate budget. The rate is an accounting assumption. A steady-state spectrum cannot validate operation at that rate.</p>
+        </section>
+        <details class="card ol-card">
+          <summary><strong>Physics, scope and research evidence</strong></summary>
+          <ul class="steps">
+            <li><strong>Passive filtering.</strong> A fixed ring routes existing light according to wavelength. Sweep the probe with zero drive to see the through notch and drop peak. Input power scales the output in this linear regime.</li>
+            <li><strong>Electro-optic logic.</strong> Chip 11 detects A and B electrically and assumes a silicon carrier-depletion resonance shift of 130 pm/V. Coupled-mode theory models intrinsic and coupling loss. The ideal symmetric combiner adds complex fields with matched external path phases by default. The phase-step control adds a relative phase ramp across the four paths. Its unused ports carry the rest of the selected power. Loss of phase match, electrical bandwidth and noise require separate characterization.</li>
+            <li><strong>All-optical nonlinear logic.</strong> An optical control can shift a resonance through Kerr, free-carrier or thermal effects. Each mechanism has its own dynamics and loss. This mode would need an optical control path, a calibrated nonlinear model and independent power, speed and regeneration tests.</li>
